We show the _Chandra_ X-ray image of the field around MWC 656 in Figure 1, together with the contours of the previous _XMM-Newton_ image reported in Munar-Adrover et al. (2014). Inspection of the X-ray image superimposing optical positions from the USNO-B1.0 catalog (Monet et al., 2003) reveals a systematic offset. Using three common sources we have applied a correction of RA =+0\\farcs 5 and Dec =+0\\farcs 1 to the _Chandra_ data (and of RA =+2\\farcs 4 and Dec =+4\\farcs 9 to the _XMM-Newton_ data using the only common source). The better angular resolution of _Chandra_ has allowed us to resolve the _XMM-Newton_ source into two sources. We have used the wavdetect algorithm to obtain the significance and positions of both sources. MWC 656 is detected at the 8.5\\sigma confidence level (c.l.), with equatorial coordinates (after correction) RA =22^{\\rm h}42^{\\rm m}57\\fs 27\\pm 0\\fs 01, Dec =44\\arcdeg 43\\arcmin 18\\farcs 5\\pm 0\\farcs 1 (uncertainties are statistical only and at 1\\sigma c.l.). The position of MWC 656 from _Gaia_ data at the epoch of the _Chandra_ observation (epoch=2015.56) following Gaia Collaboration et al. (2016) and Gaia Collaboration (2016) is: RA =22^{\\rm h}42^{\\rm m}57\\fs 29718\\pm 0\\fs 00002, Dec =44\\arcdeg 43\\arcmin 18\\farcs 2099\\pm 0\\farcs 0002. The offset between both positions is 0\\farcs 4\\ \\pm\\ 0\\farcs 1_{\\rm stat}\\ \\pm\\ 0\\farcs 1_{\\rm syst}, and the _Chandra_ source is thus the counterpart of MWC 656.

The new source, located \\sim13\\farcs5 to the southeast of MWC 656, is detected at a 15.9\\sigma c.l. and has equatorial coordinates (after correction) RA =22^{\\rm h}42^{\\rm m}57\\fs 969\\pm 0\\fs 005, Dec =44\\arcdeg 43\\arcmin 07\\farcs 34\\pm 0\\farcs 05, and thus is named CXOU J224257.9+444307 (CXOU-1 from now on). The _Chandra_ image reveals that MWC 656 is fainter than CXOU-1 (see below), while the shape of the _XMM-Newton_ contours reveals that in 2013 June MWC 656 was brighter than CXOU-1.

